// The claim's calls (tickets-patterns syntax.tex, Kinds: claim and fix): what the triage and the repair step say.
// What the delivery is about (ticket, kind, step, who) is in the channel tag; the line saying how to answer is made
// from the head type (coordd/src/render.rs). The check is the runner's, a program, so its call has no prompt to speak of.

// The lead's triage. `assess` replaced `triage` when it gained Decide (a head that changes takes a new name); `triage`
// stays for the claims recorded before.
call assess(what: Text, evidence: Text, where: Ref) -> Accept(note: Text?) | Dismiss(why: Text) | Decide(question: Text)
  task triage
  template
    A defect is claimed.
    What: {{ what }}
    Where: {{ where }}
    The evidence, in the claimant's words:
    {{ evidence }}

    Is this a defect to fix now? If it is, say anything the fixer should know. If it is not, say why. If it turns
    on what the right behaviour is, which only the owner can settle, ask the owner that question instead.

call triage(what: Text, evidence: Text, where: Ref) -> Accept(note: Text?) | Dismiss(why: Text)
  task triage
  template
    A defect is claimed.
    What: {{ what }}
    Where: {{ where }}
    The evidence, in the claimant's words:
    {{ evidence }}

    Is this a defect to fix now? If it is, say anything the fixer should know. If it is not, say why.

// The fix, built on what seek.call's `seek` found (FOUND, a trailing optional: a fix recorded before `seek` gives
// none, and its step renders as it did). An `{{ if }}` keeps its line, a match's tags take theirs: FOUND's `{{ if }}`
// and its `{{ end }}` share their lines with the match's, so a FOUND of none leaves no line behind.
call repair(what: Text, evidence: Text, where: Ref, note: Text?, branch: Ref?, found: Found?) -> Commit(commit: Ref) | Cannot(why: Text)
  task repair
  template
    This defect was accepted for fixing, and you are to fix it:
    What: {{ what }}
    Where: {{ where }}
    The evidence:
    {{ evidence }}
    {{ if note }}
    The one who accepted it added:
    {{ note }}
    {{ end }}
    {{ if found }}{{ match found }}
    {{ Extends(pieces) }}
    You found what already does part of it:
    {{ pieces }}
    Extend those pieces, and add no second copy of any of them beside it. Say in the commit message which piece each
    change extends, as "extends X at file:line".
    {{ Nothing(searched) }}
    You found nothing that already does part of it. You searched:
    {{ searched }}
    {{ end }}
    {{ end }}Fix it as its own commit, and check that the defect is gone before you answer.
    {{ if branch }}
    The fix is checked on branch {{ branch }}: it must be there when you answer.
    {{ end }}
    Answer with the commit, or say why it cannot be fixed.

call check(commit: Ref, branch: Ref?) -> Green | Red(log: Text)
  task check
  template
    Check {{ commit }}.
    {{ if branch }}
    It is to be on branch {{ branch }}.
    {{ end }}

// The two reviews of a fix (S1b), each by a short-lived agent with tools made for it (`step fresh`), shown only its
// arguments (`blind`): the claim and the commit, by reference; it reads the commit in the repository itself. Its
// model: any of the classes (`where model >= small`), the policy's model-for choosing among them (the system's
// policy: the cheapest); before the model classes it was the model of whose work it is, else haiku.
call fixes(what: Text, evidence: Text, commit: Ref, branch: Ref?) where model >= small -> Fixes | DoesNot(why: Text) | Unclear
  blind
  task review
  template
    A defect was claimed, and a commit is said to fix it.
    What: {{ what }}
    The evidence:
    {{ evidence }}
    The commit: {{ commit }}
    {{ if branch }}
    It is to be on branch {{ branch }}.
    {{ end }}

    Read the commit and check, by running what shows the defect, whether it fixes the defect. Answer Fixes; or
    DoesNot, with why; or Unclear, if you cannot tell.

call better(what: Text, evidence: Text, commit: Ref, branch: Ref?) where model >= small -> Best | Better(how: Text)
  blind
  task review
  template
    A defect was claimed, and a commit is said to fix it.
    What: {{ what }}
    The evidence:
    {{ evidence }}
    The commit: {{ commit }}
    {{ if branch }}
    It is to be on branch {{ branch }}.
    {{ end }}

    Read the commit. Is it the best fix you can see, or is there a clearly better one (smaller, at the cause rather
    than the symptom)? Does it add a second copy of something that already exists in the repository, beside it
    rather than extending it? Answer Best; or Better, with how (for a second copy: "extend X at file:line").

// The fixer's revision after a review.
call revise(commit: Ref, why: Text?, how: Text?) -> Commit(commit: Ref) | Cannot(why: Text)
  task repair
  template
    Your commit {{ commit }} was reviewed.
    {{ if why }}
    It does not fix the defect: {{ why }}
    {{ end }}
    {{ if how }}
    A better fix was suggested: {{ how }}
    {{ end }}
    Revise it as a new commit on your branch, check that the defect is gone, and answer with the new commit, or say
    why it cannot be done.
